Nitrate Wake

Ingredients: POST-ROCK + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Keyboards  
Creative Catalyst: Projectionists stranded on graveyard shifts scored nonexistent silent films on salvage keyboards to keep from going mad in the dark.

Nitrate Wake is a cinematic, subterranean collision of slow-burning post-rock dynamics and gothic keyboard miniatures. It pairs the glacial, room-shaking crescendos of bowed electric guitars and booming floor toms with the eerie, desiccated textures of antique harmoniums, felted upright pianos, and degrading Mellotron tape loops. The result sounds like a monolithic, 1920s funeral mass played behind an unlit cinema screen, where long stretches of dust-choked quietude detonate into terrifying, overdriven walls of reverbed sound.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: Glacial and suspended; typically 48–66 BPM. The rhythmic pulse leans on deliberate 3/4 and 6/8 waltz time signatures that often dissolve entirely into unmetered, tape-delayed swells before snapping into catastrophic, driving downbeats.
- Harmonic Palette: Somber Aeolian and Dorian modes, augmented fourths resolving into cold minor triads, unresolved suspended chords, and microtonal fluctuations caused by physically warped keyboard tapes and fluctuating motor speeds.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Optical Soundhead Tracking: Routing sub-mixes through vintage 35mm optical sound readers to imprint the audio with authentic sprocket flutter, phototube hum, and crackling nitrate dust.
  2. Auditorium Re-Amping: Playing dry keyboard and guitar signals through tube amplifiers placed in vacant, high-ceilinged single-screen cinemas, captured via binaural microphones at the back of the balcony.
  3. Carbon-Arc Saturation: Slamming transformer-balanced preamps to emulate the violent, flickering current sag of vintage carbon-arc projector lamps during dynamic peaks.
- Dynamics: Extreme, cinematic dynamic contrast. Tracks frequently spend several minutes hovering at a whisper—isolated piano hammers, wheezing bellows, and mechanical key-clicks—before erupting into deafening, distorted crescendos of bowed guitars and roaring pipe organs, only to plummet back into dead silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre took root in the mid-1990s across the deindustrialized towns of West Yorkshire and the rust-belt cinema corridors of Upstate New York. Projectionists working union graveyard shifts in cavernous, underfunded picture houses began bringing instruments into the projection booths to pass the dead hours between maintenance runs. They laced blank leader film through running projectors, casting blinding white rectangles onto moth-eaten stage curtains, and improvised live, sprawling soundtracks to hypothetical silent reels that had never been filmed. Performers dressed in soot-stained wool chore coats and grease-streaked workwear, gathering for clandestine "blind screenings" where bands set up behind perforated projection screens, keeping themselves entirely unseen while their massive pedalboards and dual-manual combo organs shook the floorboards of abandoned auditoriums.

Instrumentation
- Central Keyboards: 19th-century suction harmoniums, felt-damped upright pianos with brass tacks, Mellotron M400 (configured with decaying choir, cello, and flute tapes), Farfisa Compact organs, and tape-delayed Fender Rhodes.
- Rock Instrumentation: Bowed electric guitars loaded with heavy tremolo and octave fuzz, six-string baritone guitars, hollow-body basses running through vintage tube rigs, and orchestral concert bass drums and rivets-in-cymbals drum kits.
- Atmospheric Devices: Reel-to-reel tape loops, motorized music boxes, and modified 16mm projectors used as mechanical drone generators.

For Fans Of: A Silver Mt. Zion, Broadcast (the haunting library-music era), Swans (mid-tempo pastoral dread), Bohren & der Club of Gore.

