Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Postmark Devours the Address
- Story & Context: In the damp projection booth of the Gaumont Cinema, Arthur Pendelton pried open an iron ventilation grate to find a 1938 registered letter trapped inside, its envelope scorched by the carbon-arc lamp until the recipient's name was completely illegible. The bitter smell of baking vellum and ozone prompted the band to lace a loop of clear leader film onto the spool and track an opening dirge on a wheezing harmonium, capturing the panic of a message permanently separated from its destination. As the bowed guitars swell into an avalanche of catastrophic reverb, Arthur murmurs into the soundboard mic, "No postman crosses the canal after the lamps go black."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic, low-angle shot of a rusted iron letterbox overflowing with yellowed, rain-drenched envelopes under a pale streetlamp, the ink running into black streams, shot on grainy 35mm black-and-white film stock with heavy scratches and emulsion decay.

Track 2: "Why Did You Wait Until the Winter Rail Froze?"
- Story & Context: Clara Vane composed this track around a stack of thirty-two unsent postal telegrams she discovered buried in the floorboards beneath the ticket counter at Kirkgate Station. Her fingers traced the brittle paper while Julian Vance bowed the lowest string of an overdriven baritone guitar, building a tension as heavy as the coal smoke clinging to the rafters. The song’s central climax mimics the terrifying shriek of an overdue train braking on iced tracks, underscored by a fractured Mellotron choir that cuts out abruptly right as Clara recites, "You let the greasepaint freeze before you read the second line."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric archival-style image of a deserted railway platform blanketed in dark, soot-speckled snow at twilight, a single wooden luggage cart carrying an open trunk of unsent telegraph forms, shrouded in cold fog and soft theatrical lighting.

Track 3: Optical Flutter in the Dead Letter Office
- Story & Context: The final myth of the scene tells of Halifax’s condemned St. Jude’s Parcel Depository, where abandoned wartime packages were allegedly spliced together by projectionists into a single paper ribbon and run through an optical sound pickup to decode the vibrations of dead voices. Translating that urban legend into a sixteen-minute monolith, the band layered a detuned parlor piano beneath massive waves of distorted plate-reverb percussion that shake the very foundations of the hall. The deafening wall of sound finally evaporates into the faint, repeating mechanical click of a tape splice passing an audio head, trailing Clara’s final spoken invocation: "Every returned parcel is a ghost looking for a door."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A vast, decaying archive warehouse filled with towering rows of rotting paper parcels and tied bundles of correspondence, illuminated only by a ghostly beam of white light piercing through a broken glass skylight, floating cellulose dust, dark ambient film aesthetic.

